A||N||4||Taijiguy||Funny stories.........||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||04-13-2001||10:31 PM||||Witnessed a funny thing today, and thought I would share some funny stories about cars.<P>My first Fiero was an 84. It was a pretty little thing, Black, nice paint, but I'm pretty sure it had head gasket troubles-among other things, since it would devour coolant.<BR>I had just installed a fancy alarm setup, with full window and doorlock controls, and the cool "chirp-chirp" sound when it was armed. It had of course numerous configurable options, among them being auto arming when the doors closed. <BR>The day following my successful installation of this alarm, I decided to go visit my dad, who lives about two and a half hours away. Heading east on I-70 through Ohio, the pot of coffee I had before leaving, and the two I had since caught up with me. I pull my nice shiney Fiero into the crowded rest stop, kill the motor, and climb out. I shut the door, and stand proudly by, sort of with this "Bond, James Bond" demeanor, as I watch the miracle of automation equivelent to anything ever installed by Q set into action: chirp-chirp" the alarm reports it's armed condition. "Chunk-chunk" the locks respond to the sigal to engage, then, "rrrrrrrrrrrrr" the windows magically rise to enclose my precious chariot. And with uncanny timing, another, less expected sound: SSssssssssssshhhhhhhhhhhhhhh......splashhh...the sound of water draining from my cooling system, sort of as a final nail in the gauntlet of impenetrable protection provided by my sophisticated alarm system. I stood there and watched. Dumbfounded.<BR>Now, there are a couple of elements of this event that are worth mention: due to the fact that the draining of my cooling system was timed so perfectly with all of the other functions of the alarm, there was actually a brief moment where I stood there, watching, trying to remember if I installed that component or not, that allowed the water to drain. Then of course there were the bystanders close by, watching, who I'm sure wondered what kind of bonehead would install a device to drain the water from his car when the alarm armed. <BR>Now I know how Q feels when James takes one of his cars for a ride.<P>More later...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Add your own stories...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-13-2001||11:50 PM||||I dont have a story, but yours sure was funny!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||7
Z||000002||wkayl||04-14-2001||12:26 AM||||Here is what a Fiero looks like at 12,000 ft with the keys locked inside.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lovelandpass_1.jpg"> <BR>I wanted to get this shot.  I pulled in cross-ways taking up 3 spaces, jumped out, set up the video camera and waited about 5 min until there were no people in the view.  My wife got tired of waiting so she got out and locked the doors.  I was in such a hurry when I stopped that I pulled the keys and dropped them on the console.  After the shot I ran to the car and low and behold it was locked.  My wife locked it because she left her purse inside (with the spare keys).  After much finger pointing as to who was at fault, a tourist offered a coathanger.  I didn't know how to get it in the window because they were "tight".  while I was trying the drivers's side a guy popped the pass out about 1/2 inch.  if I had saw him do it I would have screamed. I stuck the hanger through, but it wasn't long enough.  Another guy had some duct tape and I took off my antenna (glad it was finger tight) and taped it to the hanger.  Success!!! <P>P.S.  I like Spell Check||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLisa||04-14-2001||12:41 AM||||Ha, OK so I go to this big banquet thing, it was an awards ceremony for a Horse Riding club I was in. My friend and I were all dressed up and about to go inside and I wanted to show her how to lock the doors to make sure they stayed locked...So I get out of the car, and lock the door to show her, and she immediately locks hers- and then I realize that my keys are locked in the car. And the car is running. And my left turn signal is on.  <BR>So I had to wait for the cop to get the door open, but meanwhile all these people are pulling in to go to the dinner, and I'm standing there trying to block that turn signal and the obvious fact that my car is running and I'm not in it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||beer_stud_21||04-14-2001||12:54 AM||||Wkayl,<P>From the picture I never would have guessed the keys were locked inside.  hehe<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
